    Bob's notification. Buys: demonstrates the real loop honestly (someone else changed it → you were told), fully
    deterministic, no timers. Costs: a current-user concept, and notification state keyed per user.
  - [1mSimulated background activity[0m — a fake worker mutates watched tasks on a timer so notifications arrive unprompted.
    Buys: the actual [3mfeeling[0m of a notification system — things arrive while you're looking elsewhere. Costs: a simulator
    module, plus nondeterminism that makes demos awkward unless I add a "fire now" button anyway.
  - [1mSelf-inflicted only[0m — you edit, you get notified. Buys: the simplest possible thing. Costs: shows the plumbing but
    not the point; every real system filters out your own changes.
  - [1mTwo real tabs[0m — each tab is a user, synced via BroadcastChannel. Buys: feels genuinely live. Costs: the most
    machinery, and it's awkward to show on one screen.

  One thing here is [3mnot[0m cheap to change later: whether notification and watch state is keyed per user. Retrofitting that
  means touching every read and write of stored state. The other three differ mainly in what triggers a write, which is
  swappable.

  [1mMy recommendation: the "acting as" user switcher.[0m It's the only option that makes "users get notified when tasks [3mthey[0m
  care about change" literally true and visible on one screen, it's deterministic so the demo repeats, and it forces the
  per-user keying decision now rather than later. Background simulation is a nice add-on afterward if you want the
  unprompted-arrival feel — same event path, one more caller.
